From: OpenWrt packaging fix
Subject: csdr.c: fix %x format vs float* argument in NEON debug prints

Two debug fprintf() calls in the -DNEON_OPTS path pass a float*
(taps) directly to a "%x" conversion, which expects unsigned int.
Harmless in practice on 32-bit ARM (both are 32-bit, so it just
prints the address), but it's undefined behavior per the C standard,
-Wformat correctly flags it, and it would misbehave for real on a
64-bit build. The line right below already casts the same pointer to
(unsigned) for its alignment bitmask math - reuse that same cast for
the printf argument instead of introducing a different idiom.

--- a/csdr.c
+++ b/csdr.c
@@ -1149,9 +1149,9 @@ int main(int argc, char *argv[])
         errhead(); fprintf(stderr,"padded_taps_length = %d\n", padded_taps_length);
 
         taps = (float*) (float*)malloc((padded_taps_length+NEON_ALIGNMENT)*sizeof(float));
-        errhead(); fprintf(stderr,"taps = %x\n", taps);
+        errhead(); fprintf(stderr,"taps = %x\n", (unsigned)taps);
         taps =  (float*)((((unsigned)taps)+NEON_ALIGNMENT-1) & ~(NEON_ALIGNMENT-1));
-        errhead(); fprintf(stderr,"NEON aligned taps = %x\n", taps);
+        errhead(); fprintf(stderr,"NEON aligned taps = %x\n", (unsigned)taps);
         for(int i=0;i<padded_taps_length-taps_length;i++) taps[taps_length+i]=0;
 #else
         taps=(float*)malloc(taps_length*sizeof(float));
